# Compute

> A bounded weekly clock offered as an alternate to continuous Unix time.

## Purpose
Explain compute as a week-shaped clock, not an endless second counter. Source: fitzyracing1/compute.

## How it works (plain English)
1. Treat the week as the bound.
2. Count inside that week.
3. Do not pretend time is an infinite Unix tape.
4. Use the clock as a work frame.

## Key ideas
- Bounded clock (time has an edge)
- Weekly frame (the bound is one week)
- Alternate to Unix time (a different tape than seconds-since-1970)

## The actual code
```
# source: fitzyracing1/compute
clock = weekly_bound
count_inside_week()
```

## Line-by-line translation
1. clock = weekly_bound → “the clock stops at the week edge”
2. count_inside_week() → “count only inside that frame”

## Decisions the program makes
- If a count would leave the week → wrap or stop at the bound
- If Unix time is offered → keep this clock as the alternate

## What a person would notice
Work is framed by a week, not by an endless counter.

## Current status
Barrage clone.

